a long while ago,

1338
00:44:46.820 --> 00:44:48.510
I kind of stopped trying to figure out

1339
00:44:48.510 --> 00:44:51.814
what the perfect conditions are for writing or what the,

1340
00:44:51.814 --> 00:44:55.180
the best place to do it or how to do it,

1341
00:44:55.180 --> 00:44:56.517
or this process or that process.

1342
00:44:56.517 --> 00:45:00.083
You just have to do it in whatever way you can get to it.

1343
00:45:01.280 --> 00:45:03.630
The fact that this is your process now is great.

1344
00:45:03.630 --> 00:45:07.390
And you know Rob Bell says every project will demand

1345
00:45:07.390 --> 00:45:10.140
maybe a slightly different process from him.

1346
00:45:10.140 --> 00:45:12.500
I look at him and this is total projection,

1347
00:45:12.500 --> 00:45:15.260
but it just appears that it flows effort effortlessly,

1348
00:45:15.260 --> 00:45:16.700
like out of that guy,

1349
00:45:16.700 --> 00:45:19.623
'cause he's such a constant font of creative output.

1350
00:45:19.623 --> 00:45:22.963
And I have this unhealthy thing,

1351
00:45:23.800 --> 00:45:26.330
which is if it doesn't hurt,

1352
00:45:26.330 --> 00:45:28.140
then you didn't push yourself hard enough,

1353
00:45:28.140 --> 00:45:29.840
and it could have been better.

1354
00:45:29.840 --> 00:45:33.920
Which is at odds with this idea that I've tried to embrace,

1355
00:45:33.920 --> 00:45:36.230
but I've been unable to master,

1356
00:45:36.230 --> 00:45:38.750
which is what if it were easy, right?

1357
00:45:38.750 --> 00:45:40.310
Like what if it just flowed?

1358
00:45:40.310 --> 00:45:41.430
Like what if it were easy?

1359
00:45:41.430 --> 00:45:45.040
What if you let go of that story that revolves around

1360
00:45:45.040 --> 00:45:47.280
the grind and what comes out of the grind,

1361
00:45:47.280 --> 00:45:50.266
and you just allowed yourself to be in a space

1362
00:45:50.266 --> 00:45:54.960
where it just comes because you're in the allowing

1363
00:45:54.960 --> 00:45:56.751
rather than in the forcing.

1364
00:45:56.751 --> 00:45:57.584
Interesting.

1365
00:45:57.584 --> 00:45:59.560
And that's the mountain that I've tried to climb.

1366
00:45:59.560 --> 00:46:01.810
and I'm still at the base of that mountain,

1367
00:46:01.810 --> 00:46:02.770
I would say.

1368
00:46:02.770 --> 00:46:03.690
Well like-
But I have had,

1369
00:46:03.690 --> 00:46:04.523
go ahead, sorry.

1370
00:46:04.523 --> 00:46:05.370
No, no, please.

1371
00:46:05.370 --> 00:46:07.240
I also get accused of interrupting you too much.

1372
00:46:07.240 --> 00:46:08.566
I think I interrupt you constantly.

1373
00:46:08.566 --> 00:46:09.686
(Rich laughing)

1374
00:46:09.686 --> 00:46:13.710
I've interrupted people my entire career.

1375
00:46:13.710 --> 00:46:15.590
At the beginning it was really bad.

1376
00:46:15.590 --> 00:46:18.430
I'd listen to the interview tapes and I'd cringe at myself.

1377
00:46:18.430 --> 00:46:19.753
It's gotten a little better, but I do it to you as well.

1378
00:46:19.753 --> 00:46:24.753
But all I was gonna suggest was that if you look

1379
00:46:26.320 --> 00:46:29.050
at sports or if you look at how you got better

1380
00:46:29.050 --> 00:46:31.566
at a certain thing in athletics,

1381
00:46:31.566 --> 00:46:35.212
has it ever been through just easing up?

1382
00:46:35.212 --> 00:46:38.020
No and that's the lens that I use,

1383
00:46:38.020 --> 00:46:41.590
because my narrative is everything that I've been able

1384
00:46:41.590 --> 00:46:44.370
to accomplish as a result of me being willing

1385
00:46:44.370 --> 00:46:46.530
to out grind everybody else and work harder.

1386
00:46:46.530 --> 00:46:47.678
Right.

1387
00:46:47.678 --> 00:46:48.511
It's not a talent thing.

1388
00:46:48.511 --> 00:46:50.059
It's a grit thing.

1389
00:46:50.059 --> 00:46:52.760
And so that's my default,

1390
00:46:52.760 --> 00:46:55.900
but I'm not so sure that that is the healthiest,

1391
00:46:55.900 --> 00:46:58.230
and it's certainly not the most sustainable,

1392
00:46:58.230 --> 00:46:59.750
because it's exhausting.

1393
00:46:59.750 --> 00:47:02.090
So I go, I do these mad sprints,

1394
00:47:02.090 --> 00:47:05.020
and then I'm depleted and then I need to recover.

1395
00:47:05.020 --> 00:47:07.770
As opposed to what I would imagine

1396
00:47:07.770 --> 00:47:09.350
or what I project onto Rob Bell,

1397
00:47:09.350 --> 00:47:13.340
which is that he's just consistently creating all the time.

1398
00:47:13.340 --> 00:47:15.370
And I'm not saying it's effortless for him,

1399
00:47:15.370 --> 00:47:18.500
but he would appear to have a healthier relationship

1400
00:47:18.500 --> 00:47:22.840
with his inner muse such that he's not,

1401
00:47:22.840 --> 00:47:24.470
like he doesn't hold himself out as somebody

1402
00:47:24.470 --> 00:47:25.530
who gets exhausted,

1403
00:47:25.530 --> 00:47:27.404
because he's creating.

1404
00:47:27.404 --> 00:47:28.376
No I think it's easier to be prolific

1405
00:47:28.376 --> 00:47:31.051
if you're doing a little bit at a time every day.

1406
00:47:31.051 --> 00:47:34.860
Like the Hemingway thing from 6:00 AM to noon,

1407
00:47:34.860 --> 00:47:36.760
and then he's at the bar kind of thing.

1408
00:47:36.760 --> 00:47:38.373
Yeah, I think Ryan Holiday does a good job of that too.

1409
00:47:38.373 --> 00:47:40.020
Like he talks a lot about that.

1410
00:47:40.020 --> 00:47:43.130
Like he's just constructed his life so that these

1411
00:47:43.130 --> 00:47:44.640
are the hours that he does the thing.

1412
00:47:44.640 --> 00:47:46.810
And he does it no matter what.

1413
00:47:46.810 --> 00:47:49.540
It's a Steven Pressfield approach to the whole thing.

1414
00:47:49.540 --> 00:47:53.240
And I really aspire to master that.

1415
00:47:53.240 --> 00:47:55.270
I have a lot of other moving pieces in my life

1416
00:47:55.270 --> 00:47:56.103
at the same time.

1417
00:47:56.103 --> 00:47:57.770
When I wrote "Finding Ultra" it was different.

1418
00:47:57.770 --> 00:47:59.010
That was the only thing that was happening.

1419
00:47:59.010 --> 00:48:01.020
But now there's a lot going on.

1420
00:48:01.020 --> 00:48:02.960
And so figuring out how to squeeze that in

1421
00:48:02.960 --> 00:48:05.620
while these other things are still on track

1422
00:48:05.620 --> 00:48:07.160
has been the challenge for me.

1423
00:48:07.160 --> 00:48:10.350
But one thing that I have done is try to be

1424
00:48:10.350 --> 00:48:12.060
a little bit more gentle on myself,

1425
00:48:12.060 --> 00:48:15.060
and rather than indulge in that guilt or that shame,

1426
00:48:15.060 --> 00:48:16.500
like why can't I do it that way?

1427
00:48:16.500 --> 00:48:18.480
Why do I always set myself up for this?

1428
00:48:18.480 --> 00:48:20.554
And why am I putting myself in this position?

1429
00:48:20.554 --> 00:48:22.876
But to just kind of let go of that and say,

1430
00:48:22.876 --> 00:48:27.280
look for whatever reason, this is my predisposition,

1431
00:48:27.280 --> 00:48:28.475
this is how I'm wired,

1432
00:48:28.475 --> 00:48:30.500
and at least for right now,

1433
00:48:30.500 --> 00:48:34.926
I'm going to embrace that aspect of me and let go

1434
00:48:34.926 --> 00:48:39.200
of trying to judge myself against some imaginary standard

1435
00:48:39.200 --> 00:48:42.100
of what a balanced person's life looks like.

1436
00:48:42.100 --> 00:48:46.890
And I think the reason that I wanted to bring this up today,

1437
00:48:46.890 --> 00:48:49.100
isn't to like talk about my personal experience,

1438
00:48:49.100 --> 00:48:51.046
but as an instructive,

1439
00:48:51.046 --> 00:48:55.270
kind of a launchpad to talk about something I think

1440
00:48:55.270 --> 00:48:56.670
is very common with a lot of people,

1441
00:48:56.670 --> 00:48:58.920
which is this idea of living a balanced life.

1442
00:48:58.920 --> 00:49:01.540
What does it mean to pursue a life of balance?

1443
00:49:01.540 --> 00:49:03.858
And is that something that we should even aspire to do?

1444
00:49:03.858 --> 00:49:04.823
Right?

1445
00:49:05.910 --> 00:49:06.936
What do you think?

1446
00:49:06.936 --> 00:49:07.769
I don't know.

1447
00:49:07.769 --> 00:49:11.170
Well, I think that sets people up for failure.

1448
00:49:11.170 --> 00:49:14.070
And I think most people have some version

1449
00:49:14.070 --> 00:49:15.080
of what I experience,

1450
00:49:15.080 --> 00:49:17.680
which is a sense that they can't live up to that.

1451
00:49:17.680 --> 00:49:19.000
They have this idea,

1452
00:49:19.000 --> 00:49:21.330
they look out into the world and they see person X

1453
00:49:21.330 --> 00:49:24.370
who seems to be excelling in their life in all categories,

1454
00:49:24.370 --> 00:49:29.300
and they say, "How can I be a great mom

1455
00:49:29.300 --> 00:49:31.960
and be a professional at my job,

1456
00:49:31.960 --> 00:49:34.580
and make sure that there's food on the table

1457
00:49:34.580 --> 00:49:38.467
and also go to the PTA meetings and all the stuff, right?

1458
00:49:38.467 --> 00:49:39.330
Right, right.

1459
00:49:39.330 --> 00:49:44.330
Like, and it sets up an unrealistic set of parameters

1460
00:49:44.510 --> 00:49:46.431
or expectations for the average person.

1461
00:49:46.431 --> 00:49:51.431
So my whole thing is letting go of that and focusing not

1462
00:49:51.930 --> 00:49:56.930
on how you can be balanced on an everyday basis

1463
00:49:57.180 --> 00:50:00.660
and all the things that you're trying to do better at,

1464
00:50:00.660 --> 00:50:03.530
but rather focus on the thing that you are doing

1465
00:50:03.530 --> 00:50:06.960
in the moment and be the best version of yourself

1466
00:50:06.960 --> 00:50:08.850
that you can in that moment.

1467
00:50:08.850 --> 00:50:11.470
And understand that nobody can do everything

1468
00:50:11.470 --> 00:50:12.551
every single day well.

1469
00:50:12.551 --> 00:50:16.500
So it's more like a toggle switch of being invested

1470
00:50:16.500 --> 00:50:17.761
in what you're doing in the moment,

1471
00:50:17.761 --> 00:50:19.980
and then switching gears to the next thing.

1472
00:50:19.980 --> 00:50:21.089
Like I can't,

1473
00:50:21.089 --> 00:50:24.090
first of all, multitasking is a misnomer.

1474
00:50:24.090 --> 00:50:25.570
There's no such thing as multitasking,

1475
00:50:25.570 --> 00:50:27.962
but we still have this idea that we like switch gears

1476
00:50:27.962 --> 00:50:29.957
and go back and forth from other things.

1477
00:50:29.957 --> 00:50:31.960
And some people are better at that than others.

1478
00:50:31.960 --> 00:50:33.640
And I've learned that I'm not good at that.

1479
00:50:33.640 --> 00:50:35.864
I'm good at doing one thing well,

1480
00:50:35.864 --> 00:50:39.720
and then slowly moving to the next thing and focusing

1481
00:50:39.720 --> 00:50:40.950
on doing that thing well.

1482
00:50:40.950 --> 00:50:42.986
But I can't toggle switch back and forth

1483
00:50:42.986 --> 00:50:45.720
within a single day.

1484
00:50:45.720 --> 00:50:46.999
My wife is very good at that.

1485
00:50:46.999 --> 00:50:48.980
Greg Anzalone is very good at that.

1486
00:50:48.980 --> 00:50:50.360
It's a different set of skill,

1487
00:50:50.360 --> 00:50:54.720
so it's about understanding what your strengths are

1488
00:50:54.720 --> 00:50:55.860
and what your weaknesses are,

1489
00:50:55.860 --> 00:51:00.317
and then trying to orient your life around addressing those

1490
00:51:00.317 --> 00:51:04.290
rather than guilting yourself into why you can't do it

1491
00:51:04.290 --> 00:51:05.883
like somebody else is doing it.

1492
00:51:07.640 --> 00:51:09.096
Maybe that's your balance.

1493
00:51:09.096 --> 00:51:13.054
Your balance is I do one thing at a time really well.

1494
00:51:13.054 --> 00:51:15.160
And that's how you feel the most balanced.

1495
00:51:15.160 --> 00:51:16.820
Like what does it even mean to be balanced?

1496
00:51:16.820 --> 00:51:19.410
Does it mean that you can do X, Y and Z

1497
00:51:19.410 --> 00:51:21.350
at this incredibly high level?

1498
00:51:21.350 --> 00:51:23.840
'Cause that doesn't seem balanced to me.

1499
00:51:23.840 --> 00:51:26.670
That just seems like (Adam chuckles) great aptitude

1500
00:51:26.670 --> 00:51:27.503
for work.

1501
00:51:27.503 --> 00:51:29.620
Well, how are we measuring balance?

1502
00:51:29.620 --> 00:51:31.040
And what is the timeframe?

1503
00:51:31.040 --> 00:51:32.780
And one of the things I'm always talking about

1504
00:51:32.780 --> 00:51:37.230
is expanding the window upon which you're evaluating

1505
00:51:37.230 --> 00:51:39.622
your ability to live a balanced life.

1506
00:51:39.622 --> 00:51:40.455
There you go.

1507
00:51:40.455 --> 00:51:41.288
If you're looking at on it,

1508
00:51:41.288 --> 00:51:42.639
if you're looking at it through the microscope

1509
00:51:42.639 --> 00:51:45.860
of your daily routine,

1510
00:51:45.860 --> 00:51:48.000
most people are gonna fall short of that.

1511
00:51:48.000 --> 00:51:49.257
I failed miserably at that.

1512
00:51:49.257 --> 00:51:52.406
But if you look at it over a long continuum,

1513
00:51:52.406 --> 00:51:57.406
then you're giving yourself larger bandwidth to say,

1514
00:51:57.410 --> 00:51:58.860
okay I'm doing this one thing now,

1515
00:51:58.860 --> 00:52:01.560
but what are the other things in my life that are important

1516
00:52:01.560 --> 00:52:04.090
that are not getting as much attention as they should.

1517
00:52:04.090 --> 00:52:04.930
When I finish this,

1518
00:52:04.930 --> 00:52:07.968
then I gotta make sure that those things are in order

1519
00:52:07.968 --> 00:52:09.999
and in the background,

1520
00:52:09.999 --> 00:52:12.649
the other things that are important need a little bit

1521
00:52:13.529 --> 00:52:14.480
of daily nourishment,

1522
00:52:14.480 --> 00:52:18.240
but perhaps aren't going to be getting your full attention.

1523
00:52:18.240 --> 00:52:19.530
But then understanding like,

1524
00:52:19.530 --> 00:52:20.490
okay, when that's done,

1525
00:52:20.490 --> 00:52:23.260
then I need to reconfigure my life to focus

1526
00:52:23.260 --> 00:52:24.120
on these other things.

1527
00:52:24.120 --> 00:52:27.403
So over the course of several months or a year,

1528
00:52:28.750 --> 00:52:30.198
then it looks like it's in balance.

1529
00:52:30.198 --> 00:52:31.031
Right.

1530
00:52:31.031 --> 00:52:32.440
But when you look at it on a,

1531
00:52:32.440 --> 00:52:34.360
like if you look at my life on a day-to-day basis,

1532
00:52:34.360 --> 00:52:35.540
it's completely out of balance.

1533
00:52:35.540 --> 00:52:37.370
So Brad Stulberg,

1534
00:52:37.370 --> 00:52:38.830
the other week we talked about Steve Magness,

1535
00:52:38.830 --> 00:52:42.327
we used his kind of quote and something that he had written

1536
00:52:42.327 --> 00:52:45.563
as a topic for discussion.

1537
00:52:46.650 --> 00:52:49.710
Steve's writing partner is Brad Stulberg,

1538
00:52:49.710 --> 00:52:51.460
who's also been on the podcast,

1539
00:52:51.460 --> 00:52:56.140
New York Times contributor, Outside Magazine contributor.

1540
00:52:56.140 --> 00:52:57.020
Have you ever met Brad?

1541
00:52:57.020 --> 00:52:57.853
I have not.

1542
00:52:57.853 --> 00:52:58.800
You'd like him, he's great.

1543
00:52:58.800 --> 00:53:01.490
But he's written extensively about this subject.

1544
00:53:01.490 --> 00:53:06.427
And one of the things that he said is that,

1545
00:53:06.427 --> 00:53:08.420
"A lot of people swallow this cultural message

1546
00:53:08.420 --> 00:53:09.610
of balance, balance, balance.

1547
00:53:09.610 --> 00:53:10.832
Then they struggled to find it,

1548
00:53:10.832 --> 00:53:12.440
and they get down on themselves,

1549
00:53:12.440 --> 00:53:14.860
because they can't do it everything every single day.

1550
00:53:14.860 --> 00:53:16.200
They think I'm going to wake up.

1551
00:53:16.200 --> 00:53:17.460
I'm gonna have some family time.

1552
00:53:17.460 --> 00:53:19.380
I'm gonna go to work from nine to five.

1553
00:53:19.380 --> 00:53:21.350
I'm gonna come home, more family time, cook dinner,

1554
00:53:21.350 --> 00:53:22.675
watch a TV show, go to sleep.

1555
00:53:22.675 --> 00:53:25.890
But that's only one way to think about balance."

1556
00:53:25.890 --> 00:53:28.330
And this is where I really resonate with Brad.

1557
00:53:28.330 --> 00:53:29.890
He says, "Try thinking about balance being

1558
00:53:29.890 --> 00:53:31.130
over a long continuum.

1559
00:53:31.130 --> 00:53:34.080
This gives you permission to go all in for periods of time.

1560
00:53:34.080 --> 00:53:35.630
That way you can experience flow

1561
00:53:35.630 --> 00:53:37.963
and still have a balanced life.

1562
00:53:37.963 --> 00:53:41.560
It's just that the balance might be over five or 10 years

1563
00:53:41.560 --> 00:53:43.000
not every single day.

1564
00:53:43.000 --> 00:53:45.706
And a corollary to that is this idea that

1565
00:53:45.706 --> 00:53:48.500
rather than striving for balance,

1566
00:53:48.500 --> 00:53:50.780
striving for self-awareness instead.

1567
00:53:50.780 --> 00:53:53.072
The ability to know your core values and evaluate

1568
00:53:53.072 --> 00:53:56.060
the trade-offs inherent in pursuing them."

1569
00:53:56.060 --> 00:53:56.950
And I love that.

1570
00:53:56.950 --> 00:53:59.050
It's also the subject of a New York Times piece

1571
00:53:59.050 --> 00:54:00.387
that he wrote called,

1572
00:54:00.387 --> 00:54:03.222
"Maybe We All Need a Little Less Balance."

1573
00:54:03.222 --> 00:54:04.055
Someone is quoted in that,

1574
00:54:04.055 --> 00:54:05.970
someone is quoted in there.

1575
00:54:05.970 --> 00:54:06.803
I know.

1576
00:54:06.803 --> 00:54:07.770
I pulled that article up.

1577
00:54:07.770 --> 00:54:09.970
I'd forgotten that he referenced me in it.

1578
00:54:09.970 --> 00:54:11.690
Just not why I'm bringing it up,

1579
00:54:11.690 --> 00:54:15.070
but I just think it's a great read for helping people

1580
00:54:15.070 --> 00:54:17.800
recalibrate how you think about these things.

1581
00:54:17.800 --> 00:54:19.464
And despite the fact that I've been,

1582
00:54:19.464 --> 00:54:21.913
droning on and on about this for a long time,

1583
00:54:21.913 --> 00:54:24.350
I found myself in the process of working

1584
00:54:24.350 --> 00:54:27.950
on this book project to go right back to that level

1585
00:54:27.950 --> 00:54:30.460
of self judgment that's counterproductive

1586
00:54:30.460 --> 00:54:31.350
rather than just saying,

1587
00:54:31.350 --> 00:54:32.920
this is what I'm doing now,

1588
00:54:32.920 --> 00:54:35.590
for whatever reason here I am back again doing this thing

1589
00:54:35.590 --> 00:54:36.970
I said I wasn't gonna do again.

1590
00:54:36.970 --> 00:54:39.420
Which is going down the rabbit hole

1591
00:54:39.420 --> 00:54:42.560
and blocking out everything else and not doing it

1592
00:54:42.560 --> 00:54:45.620
in a more managerial kind of way,

1593
00:54:45.620 --> 00:54:47.640
but this is the way that I do it.

1594
00:54:47.640 --> 00:54:48.990
And that's okay.

1595
00:54:48.990 --> 00:54:52.660
Yeah, I mean, I'm totally with that.

1596
00:54:52.660 --> 00:54:54.970
I think like this idea of balance,

1597
00:54:54.970 --> 00:54:56.580
this idea of finding your passion,

1598
00:54:56.580 --> 00:54:58.780
and following your passion or finding the one,

1599
00:54:58.780 --> 00:55:01.886
